  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely on self-reported survey data from only two cities in a single national context, which may limit how well these specific psychographic segments generalize to other countries.",
      "what": "This study segmented urban residents into four distinct parking policy profiles using k-means clustering on survey data from 1,186 residents in two German cities. The analysis found that policy-specific beliefs, transport norms, and perceptions of parking issues are the strongest predictors of policy acceptability, with support highest among 'Car-Independent Change Supporters' and lowest among 'Car-Dependent Status-Quo Supporters'.",
      "why": "It identifies specific psychological and behavioral barriers to parking reform, enabling planners to design staged implementation strategies that target receptive segments first while addressing the perceived unfairness and car dependency of skeptical groups."
    }
